From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:10 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 08/15] dndcp: Copy and paste through the Xwayland selection on
 GNOME

GNOME does not implement ext-data-control-v1, so copy and paste between
host and guest is off in a GNOME Wayland session. Mutter keeps the X
CLIPBOARD selection in step with the Wayland one, and answers any X
client for it, focused or not.

Where there is no ext-data-control-v1 but there is an X display, the
backend now owns and reads that selection instead. Host clips go on it
through GDK, with every format the host sent, and Mutter makes them the
Wayland selection. Reads use a separate Xlib connection with an unmapped
InputOnly window, and a property of their own for each read, so that a
late reply to an earlier read is ignored. Large selections arrive
through INCR, up to CPCLIPITEM_MAX_SIZE_V3.

GDK on X11 reports each change of owner of the X selection, as "changed"
in GTK4 and as "owner-change" in GTK3, without reading anything, and
Mutter sets the owner again on every change of the Wayland selection.
The backend counts those notifications and reads the selection only
after one, as it does with the events of data control. A selection that
this process owns through GDK is not read back. GDK would answer such a
read from the main loop, which the read holds, so the read would only
wait out its budget.

This makes copy and paste work on GNOME only. On any other compositor
without ext-data-control-v1, copy and paste stays off.

Tested by hand with the whole series on GNOME 50.3 (Bluefin 44), where
text went both ways through the X selection.
